1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,578
Paris, thirteen oh three.

2
00:00:02,528 --> 00:00:03,942
You are twelve years old,

3
00:00:03,892 --> 00:00:07,201
and this morning your father is giving you away.

4
00:00:07,315 --> 00:00:08,598
Not forever.

5
00:00:08,548 --> 00:00:10,956
For eight years.

6
00:00:11,045 --> 00:00:13,956
He walks you through streets you have never been down,

7
00:00:13,906 --> 00:00:16,709
to a narrow house with a wide front room.

8
00:00:16,659 --> 00:00:19,546
In that room stands a wooden frame as long as

9
00:00:19,496 --> 00:00:22,706
a door, and across it someone has stretched a piece

10
00:00:22,656 --> 00:00:23,436
of silk.

11
00:00:23,386 --> 00:00:24,896
A man is bent over it,

12
00:00:24,846 --> 00:00:28,056
pushing a needle through with thread that catches the light.

13
00:00:28,006 --> 00:00:29,340
Gold.

14
00:00:29,406 --> 00:00:31,718
That man is your new master.

15
00:00:31,668 --> 00:00:33,920
From today, you live in his house,

16
00:00:33,870 --> 00:00:35,441
you eat at his table,

17
00:00:35,391 --> 00:00:37,719
and you learn his trade.

18
00:00:37,821 --> 00:00:40,016
So let us put you in it.

19
00:00:40,119 --> 00:00:43,676
The first thing to understand is that nobody in Paris

20
00:00:43,626 --> 00:00:45,244
simply hires a boy.

21
00:00:45,194 --> 00:00:48,115
There is a contract, and there are rules about the

22
00:00:48,065 --> 00:00:49,627
contract.

23
00:00:49,693 --> 00:00:52,962
A generation ago, in the time of King Louis,

24
00:00:52,912 --> 00:00:55,908
the provost of Paris, Étienne Boileau,

25
00:00:55,858 --> 00:00:58,959
had the rules of the city's trades written down.

26
00:00:58,909 --> 00:01:00,866
Over a hundred trades.

27
00:01:00,816 --> 00:01:02,710
Each one has its own rules,

28
00:01:02,660 --> 00:01:05,534
and each one has sworn men called jurés,

29
00:01:05,484 --> 00:01:09,379
whose job is to make sure those rules are kept.

30
00:01:09,470 --> 00:01:11,243
Before your contract is made,

31
00:01:11,193 --> 00:01:12,847
the jurés ask questions.

32
00:01:12,797 --> 00:01:13,960
Not about you.

33
00:01:13,910 --> 00:01:14,989
About him.

34
00:01:14,939 --> 00:01:16,702
Can he actually do the work?

35
00:01:16,652 --> 00:01:18,831
Does he have the money to feed and train an

36
00:01:18,781 --> 00:01:20,387
apprentice for years?

37
00:01:20,337 --> 00:01:22,710
A master who is bad at his craft,

38
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:26,172
or too poor, is not allowed to take you.

39
00:01:26,238 --> 00:01:28,131
And he can only take one of you.

40
00:01:28,081 --> 00:01:30,909
A master may train his own children without limit.

41
00:01:30,859 --> 00:01:34,319
But a boy from another family usually gets one place

42
00:01:34,269 --> 00:01:35,711
in a workshop.

43
00:01:35,661 --> 00:01:36,370
One.

44
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:39,131
You are that place.

45
00:01:39,197 --> 00:01:42,146
The contract is made in front of witnesses:

46
00:01:42,096 --> 00:01:44,958
other masters, and men who work in the trade.

47
00:01:44,908 --> 00:01:47,404
Everyone hears the terms.

48
00:01:47,477 --> 00:01:49,814
Here is the part nobody tells you.

49
00:01:49,764 --> 00:01:51,956
Your family pays for this.

50
00:01:51,906 --> 00:01:53,859
The apprentice does not earn.

51
00:01:53,809 --> 00:01:55,487
The apprentice costs.

52
00:01:55,437 --> 00:01:58,492
In some trades, the shorter the term your family asks

53
00:01:58,442 --> 00:02:00,311
for, the more they have to pay.

54
00:02:00,261 --> 00:02:02,022
Sign for a long term,

55
00:02:01,972 --> 00:02:04,153
and it can cost almost nothing,

56
00:02:04,103 --> 00:02:07,759
because your master gets years of your work back.

57
00:02:07,832 --> 00:02:09,809
Your trade is embroidery.

58
00:02:09,759 --> 00:02:12,945
And the embroiderers of Paris have just been given new

59
00:02:12,895 --> 00:02:14,968
statutes, this very year.

60
00:02:14,918 --> 00:02:17,182
They set the minimum for an apprentice.

61
00:02:17,132 --> 00:02:18,729
Eight years.

62
00:02:18,802 --> 00:02:19,905
So.

63
00:02:19,855 --> 00:02:21,476
Eight years.

64
00:02:21,549 --> 00:02:24,305
Your first day starts when it gets light.

65
00:02:24,255 --> 00:02:25,371
Not at an hour.

66
00:02:25,321 --> 00:02:27,274
There is no clock in this house.

67
00:02:27,224 --> 00:02:29,180
There is a window.

68
00:02:29,270 --> 00:02:30,696
In a trade like this,

69
00:02:30,646 --> 00:02:32,815
the working day is the daylight.

70
00:02:32,765 --> 00:02:35,269
Work starts when there is enough light to see a

71
00:02:35,219 --> 00:02:37,615
thread, and it stops when there is not.

72
00:02:37,565 --> 00:02:39,709
In June, that is a long day.

73
00:02:39,659 --> 00:02:41,756
In December, it is a short one,

74
00:02:41,706 --> 00:02:45,099
and nothing anyone does will make it longer.

75
00:02:45,189 --> 00:02:48,423
Because here is the rule that tells you everything about

76
00:02:48,373 --> 00:02:49,153
this job.

77
00:02:49,103 --> 00:02:53,621
The embroiderers of Paris are forbidden to work by candlelight.

78
00:02:53,723 --> 00:02:56,624
Not because your master is kind.

79
00:02:56,574 --> 00:02:59,737
Because the provost has decided that work done by a

80
00:02:59,687 --> 00:03:02,708
candle is worse than work done by day,

81
00:03:02,658 --> 00:03:06,840
and Paris embroidery is not allowed to be worse.

82
00:03:06,942 --> 00:03:10,607
Night work is banned in many trades in this city.

83
00:03:10,557 --> 00:03:12,941
A candle hides mistakes.

84
00:03:12,891 --> 00:03:14,569
Daylight shows them.

85
00:03:14,519 --> 00:03:17,741
And the rules care very much about what other people

86
00:03:17,691 --> 00:03:19,193
will see.

87
00:03:19,266 --> 00:03:22,072
So you will not touch the silk today.

88
00:03:22,022 --> 00:03:24,872
You will not touch it for a long time.

89
00:03:24,822 --> 00:03:26,512
You fetch things.

90
00:03:26,462 --> 00:03:28,307
You wind thread.

91
00:03:28,257 --> 00:03:30,848
And mostly, you watch.

92
00:03:30,798 --> 00:03:34,143
Watching is the work of the first year.

93
00:03:34,173 --> 00:03:36,118
Your week has a shape.

94
00:03:36,068 --> 00:03:37,771
Six days at the frame,

95
00:03:37,721 --> 00:03:40,690
and then Sunday, when nobody works.

96
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:43,001
Feast days, nobody works.

97
00:03:42,951 --> 00:03:46,296
The church calendar is the calendar of work.

98
00:03:46,326 --> 00:03:48,183
And Saturday ends early.

99
00:03:48,133 --> 00:03:49,799
In most trades in Paris,

100
00:03:49,749 --> 00:03:52,959
work stops on Saturday at the first bell of vespers,

101
00:03:52,909 --> 00:03:54,443
in the late afternoon.

102
00:03:54,393 --> 00:03:56,574
When that bell rings across the roofs,

103
00:03:56,524 --> 00:04:00,719
the needle goes down, wherever it is in the stitch.

104
00:04:00,808 --> 00:04:04,345
Then, one morning, there is a knock at the door.

105
00:04:04,435 --> 00:04:05,359
Jurés.

106
00:04:05,309 --> 00:04:08,483
Men from the trade, sworn to inspect.

107
00:04:08,433 --> 00:04:11,272
They have the right to walk into any workshop of

108
00:04:11,222 --> 00:04:13,175
their craft and look at the work.

109
00:04:13,125 --> 00:04:14,001
The thread.

110
00:04:13,951 --> 00:04:15,126
The materials.

111
00:04:15,076 --> 00:04:16,673
The finish.

112
00:04:16,739 --> 00:04:19,387
If something is wrong, they can seize it.

113
00:04:19,337 --> 00:04:21,303
The master pays a fine.

114
00:04:21,253 --> 00:04:23,912
A bad piece is not a private mistake.

115
00:04:23,862 --> 00:04:25,663
It is evidence.

116
00:04:25,729 --> 00:04:27,108
This is not a raid.

117
00:04:27,058 --> 00:04:29,573
This is the trade doing its job.

118
00:04:29,523 --> 00:04:32,542
Every piece that leaves this house carries the name of

119
00:04:32,492 --> 00:04:34,672
every embroiderer in Paris.

120
00:04:34,622 --> 00:04:36,335
If a buyer is cheated here,

121
00:04:36,285 --> 00:04:39,427
he will not trust the next workshop either.

122
00:04:39,493 --> 00:04:42,152
Your master stands very still while they look.

123
00:04:42,102 --> 00:04:44,789
You learn something about him that day.

124
00:04:44,862 --> 00:04:48,304
The years pass the way years do in a workshop.

125
00:04:48,254 --> 00:04:50,697
Slowly, then all at once.

126
00:04:50,647 --> 00:04:53,079
One day you are allowed to hold the needle.

127
00:04:53,029 --> 00:04:55,557
Then to work a plain part of the background.

128
00:04:55,507 --> 00:04:59,139
Then a part someone will actually look at.

129
00:04:59,229 --> 00:05:01,098
And at the end of eight years,

130
00:05:01,048 --> 00:05:02,630
you are twenty.

131
00:05:02,580 --> 00:05:04,068
You are not a master.

132
00:05:04,018 --> 00:05:06,010
You are a journeyman.

133
00:05:06,076 --> 00:05:08,472
A journeyman is paid by the day.

134
00:05:08,422 --> 00:05:11,440
He can work for any master who will hire him.

135
00:05:11,390 --> 00:05:13,127
For the first time in your life,

136
00:05:13,077 --> 00:05:16,327
you can leave a house because you want to.

137
00:05:16,393 --> 00:05:18,490
To become a master yourself,

138
00:05:18,440 --> 00:05:20,428
you need three things.

139
00:05:20,378 --> 00:05:22,930
The skill, which you now have.

140
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:25,228
The money to set up your own workshop,

141
00:05:25,178 --> 00:05:27,298
which you almost certainly do not.

142
00:05:27,248 --> 00:05:29,837
And the oath, sworn before the jurés,

143
00:05:29,787 --> 00:05:32,386
that you will keep the rules of the trade.

144
00:05:32,336 --> 00:05:35,175
In some trades, you also have to buy the right

145
00:05:35,125 --> 00:05:36,938
to work at all.

146
00:05:37,052 --> 00:05:40,321
So most journeymen stay journeymen for a long time.

147
00:05:40,271 --> 00:05:42,372
Some of them, forever.

148
00:05:42,486 --> 00:05:45,659
But think back to the twelve-year-old who walked into this

149
00:05:45,609 --> 00:05:46,378
room.

150
00:05:46,328 --> 00:05:49,011
You could not thread a needle with gold.

151
00:05:48,961 --> 00:05:52,162
You did not know what a juré was.

152
00:05:52,276 --> 00:05:55,354
Now you know what Paris embroidery is worth,

153
00:05:55,304 --> 00:05:56,974
and why.

154
00:05:57,075 --> 00:05:59,160
And every evening, for eight years,

155
00:05:59,110 --> 00:06:00,501
when the window went grey,

156
00:06:00,451 --> 00:06:02,188
you put the needle down.

157
00:06:02,138 --> 00:06:03,660
Nobody lit a candle.

158
00:06:03,610 --> 00:06:05,911
Not because candles were expensive.

159
00:06:05,861 --> 00:06:08,488
Because that was the law.

160
00:06:08,554 --> 00:06:11,057
That is what an ordinary life is.

161
00:06:11,007 --> 00:06:11,931
Not small.

162
00:06:11,881 --> 00:06:15,405
Just made, very carefully, in daylight.

163
00:06:15,472 --> 00:06:17,089
And if you want more of these,

164
00:06:17,039 --> 00:06:19,220
one ordinary life at a time,

165
00:06:19,170 --> 00:06:23,162
subscribing is the whole business model of this channel.
